\use{document}
\use{math}

\title {Mathematics with the \texttt{math} package}
\author {The texish project}
\date {June 2026}
\maketitle

The \texttt{math} package collects the amsmath constructions a working document
needs. Most are built into the engine and need no package; loading \texttt{math}
adds the operator and connective names amsmath defines on top.

\section {Aligned equations}

The \texttt{aligned} environment lines a block of equations up on their relation
symbols. The \texttt{\\&} marks the alignment column, just before the relation:

$$\begin{aligned}
  (a + b)^2 &= a^2 + 2ab + b^2 \\
            &= a^2 + b^2 + 2ab.
\end{aligned}$$

A chain of reasoning reads naturally with the implication arrows:

$$x^2 = 4 \implies x = 2 \;\text{or}\; x = -2 \iff |x| = 2.$$

\section {Fractions and binomials}

The display fractions \texttt{\\dfrac} and \texttt{\\tfrac} fix the size of a
fraction regardless of where it sits, and \texttt{\\binom} stacks a binomial
coefficient:

$$\dfrac{1}{1 + \dfrac{1}{1 + \dfrac{1}{x}}}
  \qquad
  \binom{n}{k} = \dfrac{n!}{k!\,(n - k)!}
  \qquad
  \tfrac{1}{2}.$$

\section {Matrices}

The matrix environments differ only in their fences --- parentheses, brackets,
bars or braces:

$$A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{pmatrix}
  \qquad
  \det A = \begin{vmatrix} 1 & 2 \\ 3 & 4 \end{vmatrix} = -2
  \qquad
  \begin{Bmatrix} x \\ y \end{Bmatrix}.$$

Like the plain \texttt{matrix}, a \texttt{smallmatrix} carries no fences of its
own, so you bracket it yourself; it sets compactly inside a line of text, as in
$(\begin{smallmatrix} a & b \\ c & d \end{smallmatrix})$.

\section {Cases}

A piecewise definition uses the \texttt{cases} environment, whose rows are set
flush left under a single brace:

$$|x| = \begin{cases} \phantom{-}x & x \ge 0, \\ -x & x < 0. \end{cases}$$

\section {Operators, stacks and boxes}

Custom operators are set upright with \texttt{\\operatorname}, while the inverse
and modular forms come ready-made:

$$\arctan x = \int_0^x \frac{dt}{1 + t^2}
  \qquad
  15 \bmod 4 = 3
  \qquad
  17 \equiv 2 \pmod{5}
  \qquad
  \operatorname{argmax}_{x} f(x).$$

Here \texttt{\\bmod} is a true binary operator (a medium space on each side),
while \texttt{\\pmod} parenthesises the modulus at the end of a congruence.

The \texttt{\\overset} and \texttt{\\underset} commands stack an annotation on a
base, \texttt{\\substack} stacks the lines of a many-line subscript, and
\texttt{\\boxed} rings a result:

$$A \overset{\text{def}}{=} B
  \qquad
  \sum_{\substack{0 < i < n \\ i \ne k}} a_i
  \qquad
  \underset{n \to \infty}{\lim} \frac{1}{n} = 0
  \qquad
  \boxed{\,E = mc^2\,}.$$

\vfill\eject
